Our Dragonfly is the escape. You track the exchange in motion so you can see where you’re about to get pulled in, and where you still have room to move.

“You’ve been quiet today.” You hear a dig. You start to defend yourself. They hear your defense as hostility. Now both of you are responding to a fight that began with a guess. The escape can happen right there. You can ask, “Did you want my take?” You can say, “I was thinking.” You can let the remark pass. And if you’re already three replies deep, you can still say, “I took that as a dig. Did I read you right?”

You can escape your own appraisal, too. The moment you see “they’re coming at me” as one possible reading, you get some of your options back. You might stay in the room and leave the fight.

Follow the line far enough to see where you can let it go.